<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Final exam problems on the cross product
-->
<worksheet xml:id="worksheet-exercises-cross-product" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
<title>Exercises for <xref ref="section-cross-product" text="title"/></title>

<introduction>
<p>
These problems use the cross product and the triple product. If you would like to review this topic before
attempting them, see <xref ref="section-cross-product" text="title"/>.
</p>
</introduction>

<exercise xml:id="rw23-9-a">
<title>True or False</title>
<statement correct="no">
<p>
If <m>\vec u</m> and <m>\vec v</m> are two non-zero vectors in
<m>\R^3</m>, then
<m>\vec u \cdot (\vec u \times \vec v)
= \|\vec u\|^2 + \vec u \cdot \vec v</m>.
</p>
</statement>
<feedback>
<p>
Counterexample: take
<m>\vec u = \mathbf i</m> and <m>\vec v = \mathbf i</m>. Then
<md>
\mathbf i \cdot
\underbrace{(\mathbf i \times \mathbf i)}_{\vec 0} = 0,
\quad\text{whereas}\quad
\|\mathbf i\|^2 + \mathbf i \cdot \mathbf i = 1 + 1 = 2,
</md>
and <m>0 \ne 2</m>.
</p>
</feedback>
</exercise>

</worksheet>
